# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# DISABLED ON PURPOSE. `.disabled` because ArgoCD's directory recursion only
# reads .yaml/.yml/.json, so this file is inert until it is renamed — the same
# mechanism tabula.yaml.disabled uses.
#
# It is NOT disabled because it is unfinished. It is disabled because four
# required values do not exist yet, and every one of them is a security
# boundary rather than a setting. The chart refuses to render without them
# (see mcp-slack/deploy/chart/templates/_helpers.tpl), so enabling this early
# would produce a failed sync rather than an exposed endpoint — but a failed
# sync on a publicly-routed hostname is still not a state worth entering.
#
# A fifth, networkPolicy.enabled, is required in the same fail-closed sense but
# is a DECISION rather than a lookup — see criterion 7. It has no placeholder
# to fill from elsewhere; someone has to choose, and the render aborts until
# they do.
#
# TO ENABLE, all EIGHT must be true:
#
#   1. image.digest — the mcp-slack image is built and pushed, and you have
#      its sha256 digest. Never a tag: an internet-facing workload must run
#      the artifact that was reviewed, not whatever the tag moved to.
#
#   2. oidc.projectId — `pulumi stack output projectId` on the
#      zitadel-apps-mcp-slack stack (PR #1417), which has not been applied.
#      Hand-typing this defeats the per-project audience isolation the
#      dedicated Zitadel project exists to provide.
#
#   3. slack.channelIds — real channel IDs, which requires the Slack admin
#      session that has not happened. Note the standing constraint: no real
#      channel goes in here while the endpoint is publicly reachable and
#      caller identity is unenforced. That is a sequencing rule, not a
#      preference — the allow-list bounds which conversations a caller can
#      reach, and it is doing that work alone until caller identity lands.
#
#      That rule got stricter, not looser, now that the app manifest carries
#      groups:read and groups:history: the list gates PRIVATE channels too,
#      so the thing it is holding back alone is private-conversation
#      disclosure. Public-only would have been a defensible risk to carry for
#      a short PoC window. This is not.
#
#   4. The Secret `mcp-slack-tokens` exists in the mcp-slack namespace,
#      carrying SLACK_BOT_TOKEN and SLACK_TEAM_ID and nothing else. Created
#      out-of-band via a SealedSecret (the ntfy alertmanager-ntfy-endpoint
#      precedent) because it needs that same Slack admin session. No user
#      token is ever mounted here.
#
#   5. THE BOT IS ALREADY IN EVERY CHANNEL NAMED IN slack.channelIds, AND
#      THE INVITE HAPPENS BEFORE THE DEPLOY, NOT AFTER.
#
#      The intuitive order — add the ID, deploy, then invite the bot — does
#      not work and does not look like a missing step when it fails. Since
#      #1421 the server verifies each allow-listed conversation's visibility
#      at startup, and `conversations.info` on a private channel the bot has
#      not joined returns `channel_not_found`, so the process exits non-zero.
#      With replicas: 1 and self-heal that presents as CrashLoopBackOff —
#      i.e. "the deploy is broken", pointing at the chart rather than at the
#      skipped invite. Aegis's catch on #1421.
#
#   6. A REVERSIBLE fast containment lever exists — currently the Cloudflare
#      WAF/rate-limit rule (criterion 3, James's account). Not "a stop button"
#      but a reversible one: revoking the Slack bot token also stops the
#      endpoint, but restoring it needs a re-seal and a deploy, so under a CI
#      outage it is a one-way door. The test to apply to any lever proposed
#      later is whether UNDOING it requires CI.
#
#      THIS RULE IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN IaC, and "no managed path exists" is
#      too weak a reason — it reads as a limitation, which invites someone to
#      fix it. The actual argument, which a future proposal has to defeat:
#
#      Codifying it makes the ENABLED state a managed field. Toggle the rule
#      on in the console during an incident and that is drift, so the next
#      apply turns your containment back off — the reconciler-undoes-your-
#      deviation property this lever exists to escape. Avoid that with
#      IgnoreChanges on the enabled field and you have rebuilt
#      `accessTokenType` from zitadel-apps-mcp-slack: a security-relevant
#      setting the stack will never reconcile, reporting clean forever while
#      the live value is whatever someone last set by hand. That stack needed
#      a dedicated output just to detect it.
#
#      So unmanaged is not the compromise here; it is the only shape with no
#      reconciler between an operator and the stop button. Aegis's framing.
#
#      Narrowly scoped on purpose: this applies to the enabled state of an
#      EMERGENCY control, not to configuration generally. Nobody contains a
#      breach by editing a token lifetime, so it does not license unmanaged
#      config elsewhere.
#
#   7. THE EGRESS CiliumNetworkPolicy ACTUALLY PASSES TRAFFIC. IT DOES NOT
#      TODAY — measured 2026-08-06 (`7711924c`), not suspected.
#
#      Cilium's L7 DNS proxy does not answer on this cluster, so with the
#      policy applied the pod cannot resolve anything — including the two
#      names the rule explicitly allows. Widening to `matchPattern: "*"`
#      does not help. Dropping `rules.dns` restores DNS but then `toFQDNs`
#      matches nothing, because the proxy is what populates the FQDN→IP
#      mapping, so `:443` is blocked instead. Both ways out are closed.
#      Tracked as a platform defect in #1428.
#
#      IF YOU ARE HERE BECAUSE SLACK CALLS FAIL AND THE CNP LOOKS LIKE THE
#      CAUSE: it is the cause, and deleting it does fix it. DO NOT DELETE IT.
#      That policy is the only control that survives compromise of the bot
#      token — every other one on this endpoint is enforced by our own
#      process and is void the moment the token leaves the pod. Removing it
#      leaves an internet-facing pod holding a Slack credential with no
#      egress bounding at all, and it will look like a successful fix.
#      Escalate on #1428 instead.
#
#      The chart makes this a value rather than a warning: `networkPolicy.
#      enabled` must be set explicitly to a boolean or the render aborts.
#      #1487 (merged 2026-08-08) fixed this: the CNP now pins DNS to kube-dns
#      at L3/L4 with no `rules.dns` block, so it passes traffic without
#      depending on the broken L7 DNS proxy. `true` is the correct value —
#      it costs nothing against #1428 (no bound exists for :443/DNS-content
#      exfil either way) and buys the entire lateral bound (apiserver, other
#      namespaces, Postgres, the Buzz relay — NOT Zitadel: the `world` :443
#      rule stays open deliberately so the pod can reach the IdP's JWKS on
#      every token verification). `false` is now the dangerous choice: zero
#      in-cluster containment on an internet-facing
#      pod holding a Slack token. This criterion is met once it is set true.
#
#      Written as a numbered criterion rather than left in the template's
#      header because criteria 1-8 are the list someone reads before
#      enabling, and this is the item most likely to be acted on wrongly by
#      someone who never opens the template. Aegis's ask on #1420.
#
#   8. oidc.allowedSubjects — the comma-separated Zitadel subjects (NUMERIC
#      user ids) permitted to call this endpoint.
#
#      Added late, and the omission is the point: this criterion list ran to
#      seven items across eight commits while the chart already refused to
#      render without this value. A checklist that is shorter than the set of
#      things that block the render is worse than no checklist — it reads as
#      complete, so the reader stops when they reach the end of it.
#
#      It is NOT a duplicate of criterion 2. projectId is the audience check,
#      and the audience check is not an identity check: `aud` is caller-
#      authored — any authenticated principal can add this project to it with
#      a scope string — so issuer + audience together admit every account this
#      Zitadel instance will issue a token to. THIS is the only per-request
#      identity control. Criterion 2 being satisfied says nothing about this
#      one.
#
#      Nothing outside this repo has to happen first, unlike 1-5: the ids come
#      from Zitadel and can be read today. That makes it the cheapest of the
#      eight, which is its own hazard — cheap items get assumed done.
#
# The alert for this workload is NOT in the chart — it lives in
# gitops/argocd/platform/prometheus/applicationset.yaml, group `mcp-slack`.
# This cluster has no prometheus-operator, so a PrometheusRule would sync
# Healthy and never load. See the chart's values.yaml for the full note.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
kind: Application
metadata:
  name: mcp-slack
  namespace: argocd
  annotations:
    # Wave 2, same as cloudflared/whoami/ntfy: the Gateway (envoy-gateway) and
    # the tunnel (cloudflared) must be up before this HTTPRoute can resolve.
    argocd.argoproj.io/sync-wave: "2"
spec:
  # Scoped per-app project, not the shared applications-project — see
  # gitops/argocd/projects/mcp-slack-project.yaml.
  project: mcp-slack-project
  source:
    # Git-source Helm, NOT the OCI chart pattern tabula uses. Deliberate: an
    # OCI chart has to be published by CI before it can deploy, which adds a
    # release step between "merged" and "reconciling" and lets the deployed
    # chart lag the reviewed one. Rendering the chart straight from the
    # monorepo keeps git the single source of truth, which is the property
    # this platform is supposed to have.
    repoURL: https://github.com/VitruvianSoftware/vitruvian-core.git
    targetRevision: main
    path: mcp-slack/deploy/chart
    helm:
      values: |
        # FIVE VALUES ARE RENDER-BLOCKING. Four are still empty placeholders below;
        # `networkPolicy.enabled` is filled (#1497 — #1487 fixed the CNP, so `true`
        # is now the correct value, see criterion 7). The chart fails to render on
        # any of the five being empty/unset — that is the intended behaviour, not
        # an obstacle to work around. Fill the rest from the sources named above.
        #
        # THIS BLOCK IS THE COMPLETE SET OF RENDER-BLOCKING VALUES, verified by
        # rendering the chart rather than by reading it: with all five present
        # `helm template` succeeds, and removing any one of them fails with that
        # value's own message. Helm reports only the FIRST missing value, so a
        # block that is short by two looks exactly like a block that is short by
        # one — which is how this one carried three entries for eight commits
        # while the chart required five. If you add a `required` to the chart,
        # add its placeholder here in the same commit (#1491 adds a sixth,
        # `oidc.clientId` — update this comment again when that lands).
        image:
          digest: ""            # sha256:... — REQUIRED
        oidc:
          projectId: ""         # pulumi stack output projectId — REQUIRED
          allowedSubjects: ""   # numeric Zitadel user ids — REQUIRED
          clientId: ""          # pulumi stack output clientId — REQUIRED (#1491)
        slack:
          channelIds: ""        # comma-separated channel IDs — REQUIRED
        networkPolicy:
          # REQUIRED, and a real boolean — a quoted "false" is refused, because
          # Helm treats any non-empty string as true. See criterion 7: #1487
          # fixed the CNP, so true is now the correct value — it costs nothing
          # against #1428 and buys the entire lateral bound. false means NOTHING
          # constrains where the bot token can be sent and is the choice to avoid.
          enabled: true
  destination:
    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc
    namespace: mcp-slack
  # CONTAINMENT NOTE — READ BEFORE REACHING FOR kubectl IN AN INCIDENT.
  #
  # selfHeal is on, and this cluster reconciles every 120s with 60s of jitter
  # (argocd-cm: timeout.reconciliation 120s, timeout.reconciliation.jitter
  # 60s). So `kubectl scale deploy/mcp-slack --replicas=0` and
  # `kubectl delete httproute mcp-slack` DO NOT CONTAIN ANYTHING — both are
  # desired state here, and ArgoCD reverts them within ~2-3 minutes.
  #
  # That is the worst shape a containment action can have: it appears to work,
  # then silently undoes itself on a timer while someone believes the endpoint
  # is down.
  #
  # THERE ARE TWO RECONCILERS, AND SUSPENDING ONLY THIS APPLICATION IS NOT
  # ENOUGH. `app-of-applications` (gitops/argocd/root-applications.yaml) also
  # runs selfHeal: true and owns this Application object, so patching this
  # file's syncPolicy is itself reverted by the root app-of-apps. Verified
  # against the live root Application, which lists every leaf as a managed
  # resource.
  #
  # To actually stop traffic fast, suspend from the ROOT DOWN, then act:
  #
  #   kubectl patch application app-of-applications -n argocd --type=json \
  #     -p '[{"op":"remove","path":"/spec/syncPolicy/automated"}]'
  #   kubectl patch application mcp-slack -n argocd --type=json \
  #     -p '[{"op":"remove","path":"/spec/syncPolicy/automated"}]'
  #   kubectl scale deploy/mcp-slack -n mcp-slack --replicas=0
  #
  # Seconds, and it survives, because nothing is reconciling any layer back.
  # Restore in reverse order. Skipping the first command produces the exact
  # failure this note exists to prevent, one level up: the suspend appears to
  # work and is quietly undone.
  #
  # The alternative — revert the git commit — is correct and durable but runs
  # at GitOps speed (commit, CI, sync, rollout), which is minutes and depends
  # on CI being up. Prefer it for anything non-urgent; it leaves git as the
  # source of truth rather than a cluster that silently disagrees with it.
  syncPolicy:
    automated:
      prune: true
      selfHeal: true
    syncOptions:
      - CreateNamespace=true
      - ServerSideApply=true
